05:36.283 --> 05:42.427
[SPEAKER_02]: the tropics in the north where not much paragliding action happens apart from coastal. You've got the temperate, mid-temperate areas.

05:42.987 --> 05:50.370
[SPEAKER_02]: around the Tropic of Capricorn and Cancer, north and south examples there. Sort of that's where we are here in Manila. And most of the bulk of

05:50.430 --> 05:59.814
[SPEAKER_02]: flying is done in that zone around the 30 degree south latitude. Once you go inland away from the coast, you end up in plains areas or what I call the

05:59.854 --> 06:06.777
[SPEAKER_02]: slopes and plains. That's up and down the east coast. There's a little bit of that in the west coast as well. Most of the flying, once you're out

06:07.117 --> 06:16.203
[SPEAKER_02]: in the flatlands like we're talking out west of sydney about 500 to 800 kilometers west you'll find that it's only towing of course similar

06:16.243 --> 06:25.885
[SPEAKER_02]: conditions to what you might find in brazil or south africa and even texas for example What occurs there is that because our weather systems

06:25.925 --> 06:35.551
[SPEAKER_02]: are not that reliable in terms of what you might find in Brazil, for example, if you're flying in the classic Exiara places and so on, you

06:35.571 --> 06:43.477
[SPEAKER_02]: know, they get those four weeks where it's epic, no question about it. um here and for example texas you know they have that period in june where

06:43.997 --> 06:52.064
[SPEAKER_02]: it's it's really on in texas but not every year not every day as well as it depends where the dry line is so what we have here is very similar there

06:52.485 --> 07:00.751
[SPEAKER_02]: except now in our in our sense here not just in manila but anywhere inland uh from the east coast There isn't really a month you can say where it's

07:01.031 --> 07:09.536
[SPEAKER_02]: the month like they would say in Brazil or they would say in Texas or South Africa. Here, it can be any time really from October through to March,

07:09.556 --> 07:17.640
[SPEAKER_02]: and then it's more day length related in that sense. So when I did the world record in 98, it was in the middle of November, which was great this

07:17.680 --> 07:26.766
[SPEAKER_02]: year as well. But a month later, it was flooding rain. So it was pretty inconsistent. um some other times there's been you know big flights in

07:26.806 --> 07:33.171
[SPEAKER_02]: december and january and february have done three and four hundred k flights so so there isn't it's a it's a little bit hit and miss so you just

07:33.191 --> 07:39.737
[SPEAKER_02]: can't come to oz and expect to have you know that one epic week like they do in other places where they know it's on uh you sort of need to look ahead a

07:39.777 --> 07:46.300
[SPEAKER_02]: little bit and there's been Quite a lot of larger groups come out to go towing and break wood records and they haven't achieved anything

07:46.320 --> 07:53.804
[SPEAKER_02]: because it just hasn't been the week for it or two weeks for it. So in that sense, I would classify it as relatively unreliable. But if you're on

07:53.864 --> 08:01.486
[SPEAKER_02]: site, then there's certainly a great opportunity to do some distances if you can pick the weather systems correctly. I see. Wow. Well, thank

08:01.506 --> 08:07.832
[SPEAKER_00]: you so much for explaining it so brilliantly. I think that covered up a lot of questions that I had. Now I'm kind of running out of topics to

08:07.872 --> 08:16.841
[SPEAKER_00]: discuss. Anyways, just out of curiosity, I mean, as you said that coming in as a foreigner and planning your holiday or whatever, you know,

08:17.221 --> 08:26.733
[SPEAKER_00]: flying in advance is a bit of a question, but as a local... How long ahead can you predict good weather? Or how long ahead can you see that, okay,

08:26.834 --> 08:34.144
[SPEAKER_00]: this window looks really reliable. And now I can take a break off work or from family or whatever it is that keeps you busy. And then I can just go

08:34.204 --> 08:40.932
[SPEAKER_00]: out and hammer it every other day. Yeah. Okay. So it's very seasonal as well, because sometimes like if we have a La Nina or an El Nino season,

08:41.893 --> 08:50.219
[SPEAKER_02]: then that prediction will differ, of course. So that's something that I wouldn't like to give everyone a rule of thumb for. But let's say it was an

08:50.519 --> 08:57.084
[SPEAKER_02]: average year, we could easily pick the conditions a week or two ahead. because what you need to look for is you know how much rain has there been

08:57.125 --> 09:04.430
[SPEAKER_02]: previously if it's been dry or not sometimes the summers can be when they're very very dry like we just had you might end up getting blue days

09:04.470 --> 09:12.337
[SPEAKER_02]: every day which are not really that fantastic for doing big xc's uh but in saying that um when i have done some larger flights i've been on blue days

09:12.897 --> 09:22.680
[SPEAKER_02]: So you don't really have to have clouds on the big days. It makes you get more tactical with your classic concepts of ground sources and so on

09:22.720 --> 09:30.062
[SPEAKER_02]: rather than getting distracted by clouds. A lot of people, when they end up with clouds, they put too much theory into practice and they go slow,

09:30.323 --> 09:38.186
[SPEAKER_02]: relatively speaking. There's always there is a weather pattern that we see on the east coast that is quite reliable. That's when you have a very

09:38.246 --> 09:47.250
[SPEAKER_02]: large high pressure system sitting in the centre of Australia, punching some sort of southwest southerly winds through the eastern

09:47.290 --> 09:54.733
[SPEAKER_02]: half, maybe with a light troughing system on the eastern half as well, just in front of the front. So when we get that set up and it's very

09:54.773 --> 10:01.596
[SPEAKER_02]: distinct, that's when all the big flights have been done. So they've generally been with a southwest to south to southeasterly flow across

10:01.616 --> 10:10.500
[SPEAKER_02]: the eastern half of Australia. That brings on some moisture off the Tasman Sea and off the southern coast areas over the ranges. And when we

10:10.540 --> 10:17.383
[SPEAKER_02]: talk about ranges, we're not talking about Alpine Europe ranges or even what you have in play. We're talking about little mountains that are

10:17.423 --> 10:26.868
[SPEAKER_02]: about 1,000 metres high, but it's 4 kilometres long. So... So we do have a bit of a weather difference there between the coast and inland. And when

10:26.888 --> 10:33.933
[SPEAKER_02]: that moisture comes in with those wind directions southwest to south to southeasterly, depending on where you are, it does bring cloud streets

10:34.013 --> 10:39.817
[SPEAKER_02]: and just the absolute classic stuff that you read about in the magazines. that everyone is really chasing for. But, you know, you get

10:39.837 --> 10:47.322
[SPEAKER_02]: those days, you can get a week of that in a row, but again, it's hit and miss, you can't plan for it. You can look at the forecast charts and have a

10:47.362 --> 10:53.446
[SPEAKER_02]: fairly good guess. So I would suggest to answer your question, succinctly, seasonally dependent, we could predict good weather

10:53.486 --> 11:01.034
[SPEAKER_02]: about seven to 10 days ahead, how the patterns have been working out and how dry the ground is as well. Thank you for explaining that so

11:01.075 --> 11:08.364
[SPEAKER_00]: beautifully. I just wish flight tickets were affordable seven to 10 days in advance. It's just that it's such an expensive expedition to go

11:08.404 --> 11:16.753
[SPEAKER_00]: down to Australia and you have to book so many months in advance to make sure it's affordable. What's the beauty, Aninda, is that technically

11:16.793 --> 11:24.536
[SPEAKER_02]: you could pick any time from November through to March and say you're going to come for two weeks or maybe three weeks, for example. You can

11:24.576 --> 11:31.919
[SPEAKER_02]: always be mobile and move around to an area. Like if we've got not such good weather in this part of New South Wales where I am, sort of north of

11:31.959 --> 11:38.163
[SPEAKER_02]: Sydney, then Victoria quite often has got great weather. And then when they've got really bad weather, lots of fronts going through and wet

11:38.203 --> 11:46.329
[SPEAKER_02]: weather happening, then it's really good up here. So you can switch from the, there's basically three classic areas to go flying. We're talking

11:46.369 --> 11:52.654
[SPEAKER_02]: about, you know, big distance flying as opposed to just doing local triangles or so on. So you've got the, you know, the Victorian New South

11:52.674 --> 11:59.140
[SPEAKER_02]: Wales border areas, Central West New South Wales, and up here in the Northwest of New South Wales. So they're the three main areas. And

11:59.180 --> 12:06.704
[SPEAKER_02]: between those, you will, one of them will always be firing off for sure. regardless of what the other ones are doing. Oh, well, that's

12:06.764 --> 12:13.387
[SPEAKER_00]: encouraging. All right, so just book the ticket regardless because there is good flying happening somewhere down under. You got it. That's

12:13.407 --> 12:19.009
[SPEAKER_02]: the idea. And if you get caught out, you can always go to the coast and hang around on the beach and do some coastal flying and look at all the girls in

12:19.049 --> 12:30.238
[SPEAKER_02]: bikinis or boys, depending on what you're into. And... Sounds like a good life. I'm just curious while we're talking about weather, what are

12:30.278 --> 12:38.327
[SPEAKER_00]: the best places or websites to look for the forecast? I mean, of course, windy is a universal thing, but do you guys have something specific

12:38.708 --> 12:45.115
[SPEAKER_00]: which sends out like Australian Meteorology Department or something like that, which sends out specific alerts that paragliders over there

12:45.135 --> 12:54.819
[SPEAKER_00]: follow? Yeah, look, most people use Windy. I'm a Meteo Blue fan myself. You've also got SkySight, which the gliders tend to use. I mean, it

12:54.859 --> 13:02.788
[SPEAKER_02]: requires a subscription model, as you're aware of. They're all very accurate, actually. But the thing is, once you go inland, because it's

13:02.808 --> 13:08.994
[SPEAKER_02]: flatlands and there's a whole different convection system going on just between where I'm sitting and 100 kilometres away to the west. And

13:09.234 --> 13:15.438
[SPEAKER_02]: it's very hard for them to pick that on the models because of the resolution of the models. So you could end up in a, like having a

13:15.579 --> 13:22.584
[SPEAKER_02]: fantastic day along the ridges here and hitting the flatlands and suddenly it's very dead out there and it's stable. So you'll end up in a

13:22.644 --> 13:28.527
[SPEAKER_02]: situation where the models have shown it to be very good around where you're launching from, but where you're going to end up four hours

13:28.567 --> 13:34.529
[SPEAKER_02]: later, it actually dies off very quickly and you get to the mid-afternoon point and you say, what's happened to the day? So you end

13:34.569 --> 13:41.352
[SPEAKER_02]: up not having as big a day as you expect. But yeah, the models generally are fairly accurate. I mean, for example, I use Meteo Brew and it gives

13:41.392 --> 13:50.000
[SPEAKER_02]: you some percentages of accuracy into the future. And after three or four days, it drops off rapidly down to 20% and below most of the time. I

13:50.020 --> 13:56.306
[SPEAKER_02]: find models are very good at predicting bad weather. You can easily predict when the front is coming through or how a trough is going to set up

13:56.606 --> 14:06.277
[SPEAKER_02]: seven to 10 days ahead. But to pick very good soaring thermaling style weather for cross-country flying, they're It can give you a broad scale

14:06.357 --> 14:13.948
[SPEAKER_02]: idea of what you're looking for, but nothing beats being on the site at the time and ready to go at 10.30 in the morning when the first thermals

14:13.989 --> 14:22.748
[SPEAKER_02]: come up. Indeed, indeed. I think today we are talking about your own site, Mount Bora, which I believe has been pretty much developed

14:22.808 --> 14:32.174
[SPEAKER_00]: single-handedly by you in all its glory. So just for the perspective of our listeners, if you can just give them some idea of what Mount Bora is

14:32.214 --> 14:38.639
[SPEAKER_00]: all about. And basically, if you touch down in Sydney or Melbourne or any one of the international airports, how do you make your way over there?

14:39.286 --> 14:46.088
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, for sure. So, you know, we're about four and a half hours by car northwest of Sydney on what's called the northwest slopes and plains.

14:46.649 --> 14:54.031
[SPEAKER_02]: So the advantage here is that you can get away in the morning earlier because you've got some slopes to deal with or small ridges and hills,

14:54.131 --> 15:00.616
[SPEAKER_02]: which is where the takeoff is Mount Borah. uh it's its own plateau it's got four sides so you can take off in all wind directions pretty well all

15:00.656 --> 15:07.401
[SPEAKER_02]: wind directions and that gives you an advantage for launching a bit earlier compared to say just going towing out on the flatland so we can we

15:07.421 --> 15:14.928
[SPEAKER_02]: can start an hour or two before the tow guys get going and also fly to the flatlands when it's really on or choose to fly some flats in between the

15:15.108 --> 15:21.891
[SPEAKER_02]: the ridges here a little bit like what you get in petro heater in You know, you start off on the ridges and then you end up in the flats later on when

15:21.911 --> 15:28.114
[SPEAKER_02]: you do some flying there, as an example. So it's a very similar concept, except the mountains aren't as big. So coming from Sydney, you're going

15:28.134 --> 15:37.377
[SPEAKER_02]: to be, you know, travelling up either via car or you can take a train directly to Manila. It's about a 60-hour train journey. It leaves

15:37.417 --> 15:44.079
[SPEAKER_02]: daily, you know, early in the morning around about 9.30. From Sydney, you can catch the... From the airport, actually, you can take the metro

15:44.239 --> 15:50.582
[SPEAKER_02]: directly to Central Station and meet up with the train. All in one go, so it's quite, and it's very cheap as well. So that's a way to get here.

15:50.622 --> 15:56.984
[SPEAKER_02]: Another way is, of course, Aquanis and the other airlines flying from all the major capital cities into Tamworth, which is about a 45-minute

15:57.004 --> 16:06.028
[SPEAKER_02]: flight from Sydney. Then from there we organise airport pickups. On site here, you know, my facility has cabins and a camping area, you know,

16:06.048 --> 16:12.751
[SPEAKER_02]: swimming pool bar, Wi-Fi, all the things you expect, guest kitchens and so on. So, you know, you can turn up just yourself with nothing but your

16:12.931 --> 16:21.160
[SPEAKER_02]: glider, buy some food at the supermarket and self-cater or some people turn up with camper vans and it ends up being, over summer we have quite a

16:21.180 --> 16:28.028
[SPEAKER_02]: lot of people here, somewhere between 50 and 100 on good days or good weeks when there's quite a lot of holiday action going on. The site

16:28.048 --> 16:35.493
[SPEAKER_02]: itself can handle it quite well, you know, the very large areas of takeoff that we've got plus up areas you know they don't we handle 200

16:36.394 --> 16:44.977
[SPEAKER_02]: pilots on board and you know regular events with 150 so it's not a case of it being congested at all you know people take off at different times um

16:45.078 --> 16:52.061
