[SPEAKER_00]: connect with other pilots who are actually going down there and making it happen. But quickly, I wanted to come to the point where you said you

19:37.973 --> 19:46.639
[SPEAKER_00]: can launch 20 gliders in a row, man. Is there any other place in the world that allows you to do that even? I've never heard 20 gliders or a space

19:46.979 --> 19:58.207
[SPEAKER_00]: which caters so big. I mean, the classics, obviously, Annecy in France and Petro, fantastic takeoff areas. There's a lot of sites around the

19:58.227 --> 20:07.313
[SPEAKER_02]: world that have got large areas to launch from. I mean, another example. So there's plenty of that going on. um around the place ours is a site that

20:07.613 --> 20:14.937
[SPEAKER_02]: um you know it's an inland cross-country based site as opposed to just going for a little local flight so people come here for that so it's nice

20:14.957 --> 20:21.460
[SPEAKER_02]: to be able to lay out a lot of gliders at once and have some shade trees around as well that always helps And, you know, parking directly behind

20:21.480 --> 20:28.285
[SPEAKER_02]: the takeoffs. So you don't have to do a very large walk. Particularly now, a lot of the guys are using like triple C gliders and very large, you

20:28.305 --> 20:34.709
[SPEAKER_02]: know, submarine style harnesses. So, you know, they got a lot of gear with them. You don't have to walk half a kilometre to get to the takeoff.

20:34.729 --> 20:42.073
[SPEAKER_02]: So it's convenient from that perspective as well. And also, of course, to drive almost a regular car up the mountain and how transport service

20:42.113 --> 20:48.803
[SPEAKER_02]: operates on top of that for people that want that as well. It's such a bliss, honestly, like, you know, coming in with your comp harness

20:49.783 --> 20:56.705
[SPEAKER_00]: perfectly to the takeoff. But, you know, quickly, before we get into the actual flying, how did Mount Borat develop? If you can just give us a

20:56.765 --> 21:04.167
[SPEAKER_00]: small introduction. Like, how did you create all this magic that you have now? There's this thing called the Earth about four billion years

21:04.207 --> 21:04.467
[SPEAKER_02]: ago. And

21:07.688 --> 21:15.453
[SPEAKER_02]: a bit of continental plate movement. Yeah. mountain itself during the pre-carboniferous period. If you really need to know, the geology was

21:15.513 --> 21:24.080
[SPEAKER_02]: underwater, and it pushed up above that, so that created the mountain. But aside from that, a couple of hang gliders saw Mount Bora back in the

21:24.160 --> 21:31.245
[SPEAKER_02]: early 90s, and they were flying other sites in the region, like telecom-style tower, road access sites, and they flew in this

21:31.305 --> 21:38.571
[SPEAKER_02]: direction from a place called Tamworth nearby. They saw it, and this was before the internet, of course, so word of mouth got around. I was

21:38.651 --> 21:46.199
[SPEAKER_02]: actually looking for a site at that stage already in the early 90s to start a school and get some flying for myself and just to move out of

21:46.219 --> 21:56.228
[SPEAKER_02]: Sydney. Heard about this place, already been searching for a year. Did a fair bit of weather research and also facility research. Is it

21:56.268 --> 22:05.215
[SPEAKER_02]: available to buy? Is it even possible to put roads up there and so on? There's a lot of... A lot of technical infrastructure side due to going

22:05.275 --> 22:13.480
[SPEAKER_02]: into that. And that was in sort of 92, 93. We did a season of flying here, of course, to check out to see if it really was possible. My intention

22:13.560 --> 22:21.164
[SPEAKER_02]: originally was to set it up as a cross-country site and to try and break the world record. That was always my plan. So it took about five years to

22:21.184 --> 22:28.448
[SPEAKER_02]: get to that stage and to practice and just to figure out the weather conditions and where to go and so on. And then in between all that, you

22:28.468 --> 22:36.112
[SPEAKER_02]: know get the bulldozer out build some roads start with some basic takeoffs and then extend the development out from there uh to enlarge

22:36.132 --> 22:44.096
[SPEAKER_02]: and things you know 15 years later for the world championships to have huge takeoffs you know easy access roads large landing areas and so on so

22:44.156 --> 22:51.659
[SPEAKER_02]: it sort of created that uh from from what was nothing into something quite large in an overall sense i don't think anyone's ever done that

22:52.179 --> 22:56.982
[SPEAKER_02]: anywhere on the planet before to buy a mountain and turn it into a paragliding mountain with four takeoffs i mean there's been people

22:57.002 --> 23:04.504
[SPEAKER_02]: that have buy that have bought launch areas or the hill with a launch on it uh maybe some people have got a landing area but very few would have owned

23:04.524 --> 23:13.727
[SPEAKER_02]: the entire property and uh built you know we built 16 kilometers of roads you know for massive takeoffs landing areas and you know toilets and so

23:13.747 --> 23:21.541
[SPEAKER_02]: on so it's uh it was a big it was a long it's still an ongoing project i find it hasn't stopped yet It just seems like the stuff from the movies, to be

23:21.581 --> 23:29.046
[SPEAKER_00]: honest. I mean, who in real world goes out and says, okay, five years from now, I'm going to break a world record. I'm going to build a whole

23:29.206 --> 23:36.151
[SPEAKER_00]: facility. I mean, I feel like that's a whole another show we should do some other time, you know, like how did you actually make it happen and

23:36.191 --> 23:43.556
[SPEAKER_00]: run it so successfully? It's so inspiring. I mean, just by listening, it's like, wow, that is one way to live a very fulfilling paraglider's

23:43.596 --> 23:51.489
[SPEAKER_00]: life. yeah it's it's basically you know like any you're trying to break any records i mean you've got to just up your skill level of course and be

23:51.509 --> 23:57.954
[SPEAKER_02]: in the right place at the right time that always helps and uh just practice practice practice you know what can i say that's but as you say

23:57.994 --> 24:05.800
[SPEAKER_02]: it's probably a topic for another podcast um back in those days you know when i've when i flew it was all analog everything you know you had a super

24:05.840 --> 24:13.302
[SPEAKER_02]: basic garmin gps All your flights had to be photographed, so you always had two cameras with data backs on board, declarations signed in

24:13.342 --> 24:20.744
[SPEAKER_02]: person. You know, there was none of this ex-contest stuff happening. If your photos didn't work out, you didn't have a record. You know, it was as

24:20.784 --> 24:28.367
[SPEAKER_02]: simple as that. You had to have witnesses. So, oh, yeah, absolutely. And if anything broke down, like your barograms, was incorrect didn't

24:28.407 --> 24:35.252
[SPEAKER_02]: switch on at the right time had a gap in the barogram you you lost your record as well so there's quite a few that you know did a lot of good

24:35.292 --> 24:43.157
[SPEAKER_02]: flights so you know over the years and back in those days anyway and missed out due to a technical fault they might have flown a good distance

24:43.197 --> 24:50.422
[SPEAKER_02]: but they certainly missed it so you have to make sure not just that you were flying well but you were able to deal with all the technical issues

24:50.462 --> 24:57.768
[SPEAKER_02]: that the fai required you to have with you the whole time which was always pretty tricky. And of course, you know, we didn't have Google Maps in

24:57.828 --> 25:06.314
[SPEAKER_02]: either. It was all paper-based. Well, so anyway, we're getting a bit off topic there, isn't it? But it certainly, the site itself has offered,

25:06.334 --> 25:12.978
[SPEAKER_02]: you know, thousands of people personal bests over the last 30 years. And that's what's really cool about it. Like at every level, even the

25:13.018 --> 25:21.564
[SPEAKER_02]: high-end pilots, like Regal came here for the World Championships in 2007. You know, he'd never flown 200Ks. prior to arriving here. And

25:21.685 --> 25:29.552
[SPEAKER_02]: during the practice week, which was the famous week with Ivo getting sucked up to the clouds, miracle in the storm and all that, he did his

25:29.592 --> 25:39.101
[SPEAKER_02]: first ever 200K flight out to the West during the XC Open, just prior to the World Champs. And there was a whole bunch of high-end pilots all from

25:39.121 --> 25:46.609
[SPEAKER_02]: the various teams, France, Switzerland, Germany, and the Brits, all did their first 200K flights here the week before the Worlds. And we were

25:46.649 --> 25:53.496
[SPEAKER_02]: expecting that to be an unreal week. And then, of course, you've got pilots just at a very novice level with maybe 10 or 15 hours experience.

25:53.577 --> 26:01.441
[SPEAKER_02]: And they do their first little 10 or 20-kilometer flight And that's their personal best as well. So it ranges from the very basic, easy

26:01.482 --> 26:09.844
[SPEAKER_02]: flights that a novice pilot does all the way through to the expert level doing their PBs, but all in relative safety. And it's not as hardcore as

26:10.204 --> 26:19.625
[SPEAKER_02]: some of the places like Texas or parts of Brazil and for sure Africa, where it does get pretty exciting sometimes. Seems like stuff from the

26:19.685 --> 26:25.848
[SPEAKER_00]: dreams, man, to be honest. I mean, if a 10-hour pilot and somebody like Kriegel can go in there and push their own personal limits, it's like,

26:25.908 --> 26:32.912
[SPEAKER_00]: wow, what else do you need in paragliding? But anyways, talking about actual flying, you know, a pilot has managed to navigate his way from

26:32.952 --> 26:40.137
[SPEAKER_00]: Sydney to Manila, and he's found his perfect paradise that you have created for all of us. Once you're at the takeoff, what are the first

26:40.197 --> 26:47.221
[SPEAKER_00]: things that you look out for? Kind of what are the first indicators and first few steps after you're at the takeoff, once you make it in the air?

26:47.721 --> 26:51.586
[SPEAKER_02]: The best chip I could give everyone at this stage is get someone else to wind dummy for you.

26:57.292 --> 27:06.158
[SPEAKER_02]: That's always a good sign if someone's climbing, of course. Do you have birds or vultures which are there for wind dummies? Oh, yeah. I mean, for

27:06.178 --> 27:11.882
[SPEAKER_02]: sure there's birds. Yeah, we have the wedge-tailed eagles, there's forks, there's smaller birds of prey as well, which are cruising

27:11.922 --> 27:21.230
[SPEAKER_02]: around. But they don't normally come out until it's really good. So by that stage, you already want to be. But the big mistake that most of the,

27:21.670 --> 27:29.014
[SPEAKER_02]: let's say, foreign pilots make, especially ones that come from alpine areas, is that as soon as they feel a little puff up the face, they expect

27:29.054 --> 27:37.456
[SPEAKER_02]: it to be antibiotic or thermic and they take off and then they do like a very slowly descending ridge-soaring profile, like scratching on the

27:37.496 --> 27:45.997
[SPEAKER_02]: face of the hill, which at a site like this one here, it just doesn't work. It's either ridge-sorable straight off the bat, like it's doing 15 to 20

27:46.338 --> 27:53.382
[SPEAKER_02]: kilometres an hour and you can stay up, if it's not doing that you don't hang around on the face of the hill typically in alpine situations you

27:53.403 --> 28:00.209
[SPEAKER_02]: know you're scratching right in against the pine trees looking for little little rocky areas or any open grassy areas to get any little

28:00.249 --> 28:07.694
[SPEAKER_02]: bubble you can get to scratch back up again for us if you when you do that on the side here like we see the swiss and the french turn up here doing

28:07.754 --> 28:13.878
[SPEAKER_02]: exactly that and then up in the landing paddock very quickly because you've only got four or five hundred play with so the the trick is if you

28:13.898 --> 28:20.421
[SPEAKER_02]: don't get something immediately on launch and you don't have anyone in front of you climbing anywhere you should head out uh out away from the

28:20.461 --> 28:28.206
[SPEAKER_02]: edge of the hill uh towards the flat areas near the landing area in order to find a flatlands climb because if they're not coming up the front of

28:28.246 --> 28:35.092
[SPEAKER_02]: the hill onto the launch or onto the triggering lip of the hill, then they're out the front and they're going vertical if there's not much

28:35.112 --> 28:42.499
[SPEAKER_02]: wind. So that means that the thermals don't actually arrive on the face of the hill. And that's the mistake that they tend to do. They take off,

28:43.219 --> 28:51.226
[SPEAKER_02]: scratch around on the face. Meanwhile, there's a novice pilot climbing out like a rocket from the landing paddock from quite low. So that would

28:51.246 --> 28:57.950
[SPEAKER_02]: be the main tip. Anytime it's launchable like that is good. Usually on a good day, we'll have a queue or two popping up above the mountaintop, but

28:57.970 --> 29:05.094
[SPEAKER_02]: that doesn't always have to be the case. It's more a case of just observing the wind direction and anticipating where the triggers are

29:05.494 --> 29:11.137
[SPEAKER_02]: based on whether it's straight in or crosswind, depending on which launch we're on. But the main tip is if you don't get anything straight

29:11.157 --> 29:19.963
[SPEAKER_02]: away on the hill, you've got to head out. with as much height conserved to the flat areas where there's the low hills near the landing paddock

29:20.203 --> 29:25.166
[SPEAKER_02]: where the triggers will be occurring. Do you have anything like a house thermal or something? Because that was the first thing that came to my

29:25.226 --> 29:31.570
[SPEAKER_00]: mind. Like every time you go to a mountain or alpine areas, there's always this massive ridge or a trap or something like that where

29:32.051 --> 29:38.435
[SPEAKER_00]: majority of the takeoffs are going, are heading and gaining their way to cloud base. So do you guys have something like that or is it like

29:38.575 --> 29:45.635
[SPEAKER_00]: widespread and you can just go around and do the searching? Yeah. Yeah, it depends on the wind again. Like if it's light and variable, you won't

29:45.895 --> 29:52.539
[SPEAKER_02]: actually have anything on the face of the hill normally that you could call a house thermal. They'll be there, but it's very random. Each face,

29:52.579 --> 30:01.003
[SPEAKER_02]: there's four faces, has got its own little micro system going on in terms of very light gullies or spur lines. We're not talking alpine style, but

30:01.083 --> 30:07.928
[SPEAKER_02]: like a micro version of that. So when you've got little spur lines or gullies occurring, they're going to attract some sort of thermal

30:07.968 --> 30:13.993
[SPEAKER_02]: action. If it's in the sun, that's another consideration as well. So if you're launching on the east face, it's a different ballgame to being on

30:14.013 --> 30:21.659
[SPEAKER_02]: the west face too early, as an example, or the north, for example, as well. So one has to consider, you know, the time of day factor, where is

30:21.699 --> 30:28.504
[SPEAKER_02]: the sun on, you know, what the wind direction is. And each side is very logical in that sense. So if you've got a basic understanding of wind

30:28.524 --> 30:35.308
[SPEAKER_02]: direction and thermal triggers, then you'll look at, for example, the west launch at 11 o'clock in the morning. If the wind's southwest,

30:35.328 --> 30:41.653
[SPEAKER_02]: you'll go immediately to the southwest spur. That's the place where there'll be what you would call a house thermal, but it's just a logical

30:41.693 --> 30:50.019
[SPEAKER_02]: place where a thermal's going to climb. So the way I normally treat flying there after all these years is I've I think of it just like I'm on a

30:50.039 --> 30:58.671
[SPEAKER_02]: cross-country and I've come downwind towards this launch or towards this mountain, forgetting that it's a launch mountain, and I'm five

30:58.691 --> 31:05.519
[SPEAKER_02]: kilometres out and I'm looking at the hill from 5km out in these conditions. Where would I go to find the thermal triggering? You know,

31:05.560 --> 31:12.187
[SPEAKER_02]: that's how I look at it from... But from the perspective of about to take off. So where would I go if I was just arriving here on a cross country? And

31:12.267 --> 31:18.213
[SPEAKER_02]: that's the place to go to. So if you see someone climbing, for sure, you're going to go to someone that's climbing. If you see the wind on in a

31:18.253 --> 31:25.021
[SPEAKER_02]: certain direction, you're going to pick a spine or a lip or a trigger point there. If there isn't much wind, maybe you'll search for a trigger

31:25.061 --> 31:32.446
[SPEAKER_02]: point a bit further down the hill because it might not be triggering off the top of the hill. So they're the main tips there. So treat it like an

31:32.706 --> 31:41.229
[SPEAKER_02]: isolated hill in the middle of the flatlands, which it is, and what would you do if you arrived there? But generally, if you're a first-timer, you

31:41.249 --> 31:47.972
[SPEAKER_02]: would be looking at other people climbing. That's the usual thing. When we run cross-country clinics here, And Thermalink clinics, we just get

31:47.992 --> 31:54.576
[SPEAKER_02]: everyone to wait on launch, a couple of wind dummies go, have a bit of a chat, look at what's going on there, and then get them on the radio and

31:54.676 --> 32:02.044
[SPEAKER_02]: shoot them out to a thermal wherever that might be. And you can very quickly learn from the wind dummies in your first few climbs. All right,

32:02.184 --> 32:11.453
[SPEAKER_00]: great. You know, when I was just looking at some photos about Manila, I usually saw a lot of cloud streets. So what does a typical day of

32:11.513 --> 32:18.099
[SPEAKER_00]: cross-country flying look like visually? I mean, of course, there are blue days and stuff like that, but let's say a good day where everybody

32:18.180 --> 32:25.646
[SPEAKER_00]: lands happily and goes for a beer. How does that day look like to a pilot? Okay. So let's say it's a day with clouds because we do get days with no

32:25.686 --> 32:33.949
[SPEAKER_02]: clouds that are really epic as well. But so hypothetical day with clouds scattered across the sky. The big days, obviously the ones that are very

32:33.989 --> 32:42.952
[SPEAKER_02]: close to OD. So you've got to sort of manage that a little bit as well and not get too excited when the clouds get too big. Always mind the height

32:42.992 --> 32:51.995
[SPEAKER_02]: versus width scaling on the clouds. So we typically get downwind. The ridges here are north south aligned mostly. So if we get any wind that's

32:52.315 --> 33:01.458
[SPEAKER_02]: in that alignment or within 10 or 20 degrees of that alignment, you do tend to get a run of clouds into streeting, not due to the classic reasons

33:01.518 --> 33:08.619
[SPEAKER_02]: for streeting, like upper level disturbances and so on, but you're streeting due to terrain formations underneath you. So the classic

33:08.680 --> 33:17.303
[SPEAKER_02]: route that you do from here heading north, for example, it's lined up with the north-south line. ridges and so on. So you get flatlands,

33:17.744 --> 33:29.434
[SPEAKER_02]: ridges, et cetera. So you do end up having quite long lines of clouds that are very self-explanatory. The risk is that when you do end up out of sync

33:29.494 --> 33:38.819
[SPEAKER_02]: with them, so they might start and then they'll end up putting what I call a bit of an oscillation through the atmosphere downwind. and they'll

33:38.839 --> 33:46.166
[SPEAKER_02]: end up having a rippling effect of how they form. So one forms, the next one forms, and then as you get towards them, that rippling effect might

33:46.206 --> 33:53.393
[SPEAKER_02]: end up, you could be out of synchronisation with the ripple in a sense, so you're not catching it at the top of the cycles or you arrive at dead ones.

33:53.813 --> 34:00.600
[SPEAKER_02]: We've seen that quite a lot when we've been team flying where pilots, you know, I might be in the lead or something and going, I'm having really

34:00.640 --> 34:06.523
[SPEAKER_02]: excellent climbs out of these clouds and just one or two clouds back, everyone's in the decay phase of those clouds, even though the clouds

34:06.543 --> 34:13.287
[SPEAKER_02]: look like they're lined up in a street. So you can end up having a pretty ordinary day if you're in the phasing, even though you're in a cloud

34:13.327 --> 34:21.813
[SPEAKER_02]: street situation, then you do the usual 45 degree downwind turn and try and find, get out of the sync cycle as well, of course. But the typical day

34:21.833 --> 34:29.922
[SPEAKER_02]: like that, yeah, would have clouds popping up, had reasonable glides. I mean, nowadays you've got 10 to 1 glides, 11 to 1 glides on most of the

34:30.042 --> 34:37.571
[SPEAKER_02]: classical hiders and above. That's a totally different ballgame to what it was 20 years ago when everyone was doing sixes and sevens. So that

34:37.771 --> 34:46.097
[SPEAKER_02]: very much changes it. Once the performance got to over 10... It completely changed the ball game in the Alps as well. Same story. You

34:46.118 --> 34:52.300
[SPEAKER_02]: know, you were able to do crossings without too many dramas. You'd always find a climb on the other side. That makes events like Red Bull

34:52.380 --> 34:59.643
[SPEAKER_02]: X-Alps much more possible, having over 10 to 1 for sure. But then same thing here. You're pretty well connecting all the time. So once you're

34:59.663 --> 35:07.666
[SPEAKER_02]: up on a good day, you know, 100Ks is quite easy normally, just with the fact of the glider performance now giving you that magical number,

35:07.866 --> 35:15.370
[SPEAKER_02]: which it's funny that it ended up being 10 to 1 as well. uh it was uh something that we always shot for you know around 2000 oh my god if we ever

35:15.390 --> 35:22.375
[SPEAKER_02]: get a glider that does 10 to 1 we'll never land you know i don't know why that number was picked it was just a number but uh but funnily enough when

35:22.395 --> 35:30.500
[SPEAKER_02]: it happened and the thing is we had gliders um in the early days you know with their 10 to 1 glides and they seem to be able to stay until sunset all

35:30.520 --> 35:37.988
[SPEAKER_02]: the time now that we're able to do that for the last sort of 10 to 15 years, depending on the glider level you're at. Pretty well sunset flights,

35:38.008 --> 35:44.256
[SPEAKER_02]: you know, into the sunset, eight hours, nine hours, 10 hours are entirely possible for even intermediate pilots now, provided you

35:44.596 --> 35:52.586
[SPEAKER_02]: don't race it too hard. So that makes a good day for us. Just consistent clouds everywhere. Don't fly yourself into... Blue holes, usual

35:52.626 --> 35:59.549
[SPEAKER_02]: routine, you know, you want to watch out for blue holes on days with cloud streets because they're always where the sink is, of course. In our case

35:59.790 --> 36:08.574
[SPEAKER_02]: here, with the north-south ridge alignment, until we get to the flatlands, if you do cross the wide valleys between the north-south

36:08.614 --> 36:15.798
[SPEAKER_02]: ridges, there is a fair bit of sink there, even though there might be a few dimples in the valley floor. A lot of people end up landing in a place

36:15.818 --> 36:23.320
[SPEAKER_02]: called the Horton Valley, which is a big wide 20 kilometer wide valley going from one set of ridges to the next you've got to set yourself up

36:23.400 --> 36:31.462
[SPEAKER_02]: ahead basically so that that's always critical on the countries around here as it is anywhere in the semi-flat but set yourself up for known

36:31.562 --> 36:38.183
[SPEAKER_02]: areas where the where there are blue holes and and be able to look at that in the bottom third of your climb to look ahead and see where the blue hole

36:38.203 --> 36:49.190
[SPEAKER_02]: is coming up and start making some decisions uh 20 or 30 kilometers ahead Do you quickly want to touch upon how low do you have to be before you push

36:49.210 --> 36:56.334
[SPEAKER_00]: the panic button? It's like, okay, if I don't save myself, then a bomb out is coming in. What kind of altitude do we look at? And what is the usual

36:56.454 --> 37:02.374
[SPEAKER_00]: altitude of cloud-based on a good day? I just wanted to bring an altitude perspective into our discussion here. The altitude of cloud base, you

37:02.394 --> 37:10.857
[SPEAKER_02]: would have seen some of the flights from Timo and the guys in South Africa this year, just a couple of weeks ago. They were flying there, you know,

37:11.497 --> 37:19.959
[SPEAKER_02]: 4,500 metres, 5,000 metres and so on. But what people forget is that the absolute height sounds fantastic above sea level. But the actual

37:20.059 --> 37:30.304
[SPEAKER_02]: height above ground level was only about 1,000 or 3,500 metres. And that's an interesting number, 10,000 feet above ground, is roughly an

37:30.544 --> 37:38.128
[SPEAKER_02]: average good day at most good cross-country flying sites. And we're talking about semi-flatlands here. So if we get – our ground level here

37:38.168 --> 37:48.074
[SPEAKER_02]: is around 300. So if we get 3.3 or 3.5, that's a really good cloud-based day, like a great XC day. you know in south africa in some places it will be

37:48.214 --> 37:54.821
[SPEAKER_02]: four and a half thousand asl which is still only three and a half above ground so mother nature has sort of worked it out for us that the height

37:54.841 --> 38:02.770
[SPEAKER_02]: above ground versus the distance you're flying versus the amount of wind speed there is the best days around that 3000 agl as opposed to asl

38:02.790 --> 38:11.576
[SPEAKER_02]: you get that all over the planet um same thing in texas way so that that's a good day a lot of the big days here are a little bit lower on base like two

38:11.616 --> 38:19.781
[SPEAKER_02]: and a half um that's above ground so let's say two eight to three thousand just a little bit below three thousand meters um that's they're very

38:19.801 --> 38:28.725
[SPEAKER_02]: handy days when whenever you get to about two days with two and a half thousand meters that's when this uh stress point comes along a bit more

38:28.805 --> 38:37.370
[SPEAKER_02]: often when you get low Because you're likely not going, like at some point on the next sea at that stage with a two and a half day, you're likely

38:37.430 --> 38:45.335
[SPEAKER_02]: to run into a point where you have to manage things a little bit better and decide, oh, I'll take this climb to the very end because the next one

